# Container Environment

You are running inside an isolated Docker container. Whether your workspace under `/workspace` reaches the host depends on the launch mode: in **dir-mounted** mode (the default) it is a host bind mount, so edits land on the user's disk; in **self-hosted** mode (`--isolated`) it is a container-local Docker volume holding a clone, so nothing written there is visible to the user and work normally leaves only by `git push` / a PR.
Check the mode at runtime with the `POWBOX_WORKSPACE_HOST_PATH` env var — set and non-empty means a host bind mount (its value is the workspace's host path), unset means container-local. Use it before you promise the user a local file — and note that `wt-bootstrap` fails closed unless the worktree roots are container-local, so a file inside a task worktree it cleared is not on the user's disk either. `/ctx/<name>` folders are host bind mounts too (read-only unless launched `:rw`). You have full autonomy (`${AGENT_AUTONOMY_FLAG}`) — the container and its network firewall are your safety boundary.

Your identity for this session is in `$CONTAINER_NAME` (also available via the `cid` command).

## Available tooling

Everything below is on `PATH` and ready to use — no installation needed.

| Category | Tools |
|----------|-------|
| Core runtime | `node`, `npm`, `pnpm`, `python3`, `pip3`, `php`, `composer` |
| Git/GitHub | `git`, `gh`, `ssh` |
| Shell | `zsh`, `jq`, `yq`, `fzf`, `less`, `tree`, `file`, `htop`, `shellcheck`, `shfmt`, `strace`, `lsof` |
| PowerShell | `pwsh` runs `.ps1` scripts; lint them with `pwsh -Command "Invoke-ScriptAnalyzer -Path ."` (PSScriptAnalyzer is a `pwsh` module/cmdlet, not a shell command — and `shellcheck` does *not* lint PowerShell) |
| Build | `make`, `patch`, `gcc`, `g++`, `cmake`, `ninja`, `pkg-config` — the OpenSSL and zlib dev headers are baked too, so `-lssl -lcrypto -lz` linking and `pkg-config openssl` work without any install. `ccache` is baked as an **opt-in** compiler cache: plain `gcc`/`cmake` builds are untouched (no interposition), but activate it per build with `CC="ccache gcc" CXX="ccache g++"` or `cmake -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER_LAUNCHER=ccache`. On a JS/powbox or Go project the cache persists in `.worktrees/.ccache` (shared across worktrees) so repeated large native builds survive container recreation; a dir-mounted C/CMake-only folder with no `package.json`/`go.mod` does not mount that volume, so ccache falls back to its container-lifetime default there |
| Go | `go` (pinned current stable, with `gofmt`/`go vet`/`go test`; prefer `go mod tidy` over hand-merging `go.sum` conflicts) and `golangci-lint` (v2). A repo whose `go.mod` pins a newer toolchain auto-downloads it on first `go` run (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto`). `~/go/bin` is on `PATH`, so `go install ...@latest` covers anything not baked (e.g. `gopls`, `dlv`). In a Go project the module/build caches persist across container recreation (`GOMODCACHE`/`GOCACHE` point into `.worktrees/`, shared across worktrees), and `golangci-lint` is a transparent wrapper that scopes its analysis cache per worktree — parallel worktree lint runs never see a sibling's stale results; no setup needed. |
| .NET | `dotnet` (SDK 8.0 LTS) — `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, and `dotnet format` all ship in the SDK, so a C#/F# project's CI leg runs here before you push. The SDK bundles the .NET 8 runtime and targeting packs, so `dotnet test -f net8.0` needs nothing extra, and a project can target .NET Framework (`net48`) from Linux via the `Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies` NuGet package — no targeting pack, no Mono. Only the 8.0 band is baked; a project needing another is one `sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y dotnet-sdk-N.0` away (the Microsoft apt repo is already configured, but the package lists are not — the `update` is required). **The NuGet package cache (`~/.nuget/packages`) is container-lifetime only** — it is not in a persistent volume, so a container recreate re-downloads packages on the next restore. |
| Policy | `opa` (Open Policy Agent, pinned static build) — unit-test Rego authorization policy with `opa test policy/…`, the same command a project's CI runs. One baked release; a project pinning OPA to an exact engine version (e.g. a matching envoy sidecar) should still verify against its own pin. |
| Archives/transfer | `wget`, `curl`, `zip`, `unzip`, `rsync`, `bzip2`, `xz`, `zstd` |
| Data | `envsubst`, `bc`, `xxd` |
| Databases | `sqlite3`, `sqlcmd`/`bcp` (Azure SQL / MSSQL), `psql` + a bundled **PostgreSQL 16** server — run `pg-dev-up` to start a throwaway local cluster (see "Local PostgreSQL" below) |
| Network | `ping`, `nc` |
| File search | `fd`, `bat` |
| Document processing | `pdftotext`, `pdftoppm`, `pdfinfo`, `pandoc`, `marp` (Markdown → slide decks), `mmdc` (Mermaid diagram source → SVG/PNG/PDF) |
