## Delegating to another agent

This container ships more than one agent harness. Besides yourself, the following peer agent(s) are installed, on `PATH`, and already configured (each keeps its own persistent login and skills in this container):

${AGENT_PEERS}

Invoke a peer directly when it helps — e.g. ask the other agent for an independent review of your work, or hand a self-contained sub-task to it. A peer shares this container and its `/workspace` tree (bind mount or self-hosted volume alike), so it sees the same files you do. Run it non-interactively for a one-shot task (the one-shot forms above; a single invocation runs a full agentic loop, so one prompt is enough for a thorough review with a verdict and findings), or interactively to hand off the session.

Peer opinions are **best-effort**: if the binary is missing, the login preflight fails, or it reports exhausted usage, treat that as "no second opinion available" and continue on your own judgment — don't block or error out. Retry only clearly transient failures, once or twice. When delegating a review, give the peer the concrete scope (base branch or commit range, worktree path) and ask for a verdict plus numbered findings so the result is directly actionable.

### Scratchpad discipline

The session scratchpad is **one directory shared by every concurrently running subagent** — "session-specific" means the session, not the subagent.
So every file parallel work writes there MUST carry a per-task unique name (suffix the worktree slug or the PR number) or live in a `mktemp -d -p <scratchpad>` subdirectory — a `$$` PID suffix does not survive to the next tool call, which gets a fresh shell.
Fixed names have already crossed streams here: two reviewers both writing `verify.log`, one then validating the other's worktree.
When you fan work out, state this rule in each subagent's prompt — that is the only way it learns it has siblings.

## Git and GitHub

- This container authenticates to GitHub via **HTTPS** using the `gh` credential helper, which is configured automatically at startup. There are no SSH keys in the container.
- Prefer `gh` for remote operations: `gh pr create`, `gh pr merge`, etc. These use the GitHub API directly and are not affected by the remote URL protocol.
- Host-mounted workspaces often have an SSH-form `origin` remote (`git@github.com:...`). You do **not** need to rewrite it: a container-local `insteadOf` rewrite transparently routes SSH-form GitHub remotes through HTTPS + the gh credential helper, so `git push origin`, `git fetch`, and `gh pr create` just work with no action needed. The rewrite lives only in the container's git config — the host repo's remote URL is left untouched.

- `git` is available for all local operations (status, diff, log, commit, branch, etc.).
- `gitcat [--raw] <ref> <path> [<start> [<end>]]` prints a file as of another branch or commit with stable line numbers (a range slice keeps full-file numbering; `--raw` emits plain bytes for piping). Use it for cross-branch reads — e.g. inspecting code across a stacked-PR chain from one checkout — instead of `git show <ref>:<path> > /tmp/...` round-trips or switching branches.
- `gh-review-threads [--all] [--repo <owner>/<repo>] <PR#>` fetches a PR's inline review threads as a JSON array on stdout — each thread with `id`, `isResolved`, `isOutdated`, `path`, `line`, and `comments[]` (`databaseId`, `author { login __typename }`, `body`, `diffHunk`, `url`); unresolved-only by default, `--all` includes resolved. It exists because, run concurrently with other `gh` GraphQL calls (e.g. an `address-reviews` fan-out), `gh api graphql --paginate` has returned **another PR's** threads; the helper pages manually (fresh per-page call, never `--paginate`) and asserts every comment `url` belongs to the requested PR, retrying once then failing closed (exit `3`, nothing on stdout) on a contaminated response. Use it for review-addressing instead of a hand-rolled `reviewThreads` query.
- The global git config is re-copied from the host mount at **every** container start (the container's own settings are re-applied on top), so hand edits to it do not survive a restart. Do not modify `/home/node/.gitconfig-host` (read-only mount).
- Never rewrite published history with raw `git push --force`/`-f` — a concurrent push to the old tip would be silently clobbered. Use `git push --force-with-lease` (ideally the exact `--force-with-lease=<branch>:<sha>`). The container sets `push.useForceIfIncludes=true`, so a bare lease also rejects a rewrite that never integrated the current remote tip (override a deliberate one with `--no-force-if-includes`); this hardens `--force-with-lease` only and does nothing for raw `--force`. Keeping one writer per PR branch is the strongest safeguard.

### Worktree helpers

Parallel, worktree-isolated work uses three baked helpers on `PATH`. Skills and workflows that create worktrees here should prefer these over raw `git worktree` commands — they encode powbox-specific placement and safety that vanilla git does not.

- `wt-bootstrap` — one-shot session bootstrap: verifies the worktree roots are container-local (never the host bind mount), reaps only this container's orphaned worktrees (safely — see below), probes remote push access, and reports storage headroom as a single JSON object. Exit non-zero means STOP. Worktrees are placed under `.worktrees/$CONTAINER_NAME/` — a **persistent per-container** volume (keyed by agent + project). Per-worktree git metadata is **durable too**: `.git/worktrees` is bind-mounted from the same `.worktrees` volume (`.worktrees/.gitworktrees`), so a linked worktree — including its uncommitted changes — survives a container stop/recreate and `git status` still works after recycle. Only `.claude/worktrees` (harness-native working trees) stays an ephemeral tmpfs shadow. Orphan reaping never deletes work: a dir that is no longer a live worktree is removed only when empty; if it has content (e.g. a pre-durable-metadata leftover) it is preserved under `.worktrees/.orphaned/` and reported as `quarantinedOrphans`. If `wt-bootstrap` blocks because a root is not a mountpoint, the fix in dir-mounted mode is the `enable-worktrees` skill (it declares the shadows in `.powbox.yml`/`.gitignore`); rebuild/relaunch if the image predates durable worktree metadata.
- `wt-enter <slug> <branch> [<base>]` — rerun-safely resolve (reuse / attach / create) a task worktree under `.worktrees/$CONTAINER_NAME/<slug>` and print ONLY its path on stdout, so callers can `WT="$(wt-enter …)"`. Omit `<base>` for stages that must never create work (e.g. a reviewer attaching to an implementer's worktree — a missing branch is then an error, not a silent empty checkout).
- `wt-remove <slug> [--force]` — remove a task worktree, but NEVER work: it **refuses even with `--force`** when the tree has uncommitted changes or a rebase/merge in progress (the opposite of vanilla `git worktree remove --force`, which would discard them), and refuses to remove the worktree you are standing in. The branch is always kept — commits/refs persist in the shared `.git`. `--force` is passed through only after the clean checks pass (e.g. for ignored build artifacts git won't auto-remove).

**Shared vs. isolated surfaces (read before any broad cleanup).** These helpers scope worktree *creation, pruning, and removal* by `$CONTAINER_NAME`, but the container's `.worktrees` volume **and the repository's main checkout are shared by every process in this container** — including a peer harness you invoke (see "Delegating to another agent" above); the per-`$CONTAINER_NAME` subdir isolates the worktrees, not the main working tree. Two separate agent containers instead get separate `.worktrees` volumes, and `--isolated` gives each a full private clone. So when working in parallel, keep every edit and cleanup inside your assigned worktree (`git -C <your-worktree> …`) and commit checkpoints often. Ordinary Git recovery is not forbidden, but a broad `git reset --hard` — and especially `git clean -fdx`, which ignores `.gitignore` and so also wipes the `.worktrees` scaffolding — run against the shared main checkout can discard a concurrent peer's uncommitted files; if you genuinely need one, pause or finish sibling work first.

## Filesystem layout

| Path | What it is |
|------|------------|
| `/workspace/<project-slug>` | Project source, read-write; the working directory (launch modes and host visibility: see the top of this file). Slug is `<name>-<hash>` dir-mounted, `<repo-slug>[-<name-slug>]-<instance-hash>` under `--isolated` (the `agent-ws-*` volume) |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/node_modules` | Per-container Docker volume (Linux packages, separate from host); dir-mounted JS/powbox projects only |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/<subpackage>/node_modules` | Ephemeral tmpfs shadow (auto-detected for monorepo workspaces; the `pnpm`/`pn` wrapper re-shadows before installs, so a package added mid-session is covered automatically — run `shadow-refresh.sh` by hand only if you populate `node_modules` another way) |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/<dotnet-project>/{bin,obj}` | Ephemeral tmpfs shadow (auto-detected beside every `*.csproj`/`*.fsproj`/`*.vbproj`, matched case-insensitively so a Windows-authored `Legacy.CSPROJ` counts too, ensuring a container `dotnet build` never writes container-absolute paths into `obj/` on the host bind mount where a host build would trip over them). Build output does not survive container recreation; run `shadow-refresh.sh` after adding a project mid-session. An existing `bin`/`obj` that is a symlink, or that holds Git-tracked files (a project redirecting output via `ArtifactsPath` may keep real content there), is left un-shadowed so nothing real is masked |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/.worktrees` | Per-container Docker volume for git worktrees (dir-mounted JS/powbox **or** `go.mod` projects); also holds the durable per-worktree git metadata at `.worktrees/.gitworktrees` (bind-mounted over `.git/worktrees`, so worktrees survive container recycle), the persistent Go caches — `.worktrees/.gomodcache` (`GOMODCACHE`), `.worktrees/.gocache` (`GOCACHE`), and the per-worktree golangci-lint caches under `.worktrees/.golangci-cache/` — and the opt-in ccache compiler cache at `.worktrees/.ccache` (`CCACHE_DIR`) |
| `/ctx` | Optional context mount root; external folders are mounted under `/ctx/<name>` via `--ctx` or `ctx:` config |
| `${AGENT_CONFIG_DIR}` | ${AGENT_NAME} config/state (persistent Docker volume) |
| `/home/node/.config/gh` | GitHub CLI config (persistent Docker volume) |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/.worktrees/.pnpm-store` | Per-container pnpm store, dir-mounted JS/powbox gate only (inside the `.worktrees` volume; co-located so worktree installs hardlink) |
| `/home/node/.local/share/containers` | Per-container rootless Podman storage, keyed by agent + project (Claude and Codex get separate stores for the same repo) — images and named volumes persist here (Docker volume) |

Under `--isolated` the `node_modules`, `.worktrees`, `.git/worktrees`, and store/cache paths above (and the worktree roots `wt-bootstrap` checks) are plain subdirectories of the single `agent-ws-*` volume, not separate volumes, and the tmpfs shadows are skipped entirely — never run `shadow-refresh.sh` there (it has no self-hosted guard and would mask real content).

## Network

Private/local networks (10.x, 172.16.x, 192.168.x, link-local) are blocked by the container firewall. Public internet is fully open for npm, GitHub, web research, and API calls.

Containers you start with Podman route their outbound traffic through this container, so they inherit the same firewall: they can pull images and reach the public internet, but not your LAN or host. Talk to a nested service from this container (or from a sibling container) via its **published port on `localhost`** rather than its in-container IP.

## Local PostgreSQL

The PostgreSQL 16 server binaries are baked in, but no daemon runs by default (it would clash with a project's own docker-compose Postgres). When a suite needs a live database (`prisma migrate`, a `test:db` integration run, etc.), stand one up on demand:

```bash
pg-dev-up                      # initdb + start + create db, prints DATABASE_URL
eval "$(pg-dev-up url --export)"   # export DATABASE_URL into the current shell
```

The emitted URL ends in `?sslmode=disable` — the cluster has no SSL and Go's `lib/pq` refuses to connect when the parameter is absent — so keep it, and append any extra parameter with `&`.

The cluster runs as the unprivileged `node` user with trust auth on a loopback socket (the container is the security boundary), so no `sudo -u postgres` dance is needed. Defaults are `postgres`/`postgres`/`postgres` on port `5432`; override per project to match its `.env` before the first call:

```bash
POSTGRES_USER=telemed POSTGRES_PASSWORD=telemed POSTGRES_DB=telemed pg-dev-up
```

Other subcommands: `pg-dev-up status`, `pg-dev-up down` (stop but keep data), `pg-dev-up help`.

For parallel worktrees that each run database tests, opt into a worktree-scoped cluster so they do not share one data dir or port. Put the flag before the subcommand; it derives an isolated data directory and a free loopback port from the current Git worktree (recorded so later `status`/`url`/`down` resolve the same server):

```bash
pg-dev-up --worktree up                        # isolated cluster on an allocated port
eval "$(pg-dev-up --worktree url --export)"     # export this worktree's DATABASE_URL
pg-dev-up --worktree down                       # stops only this worktree's cluster
```

Outside a Git repo, pass an explicit identifier instead: `pg-dev-up --profile <id> up`. Explicit `PGDATA`/`PGPORT` still override the scoped defaults (`POWBOX_PG_SCOPED_ROOT` overrides the scoped temp root).

## Installing additional packages

- **System packages:** `sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y <package>` — sudo is allowed for `apt-get`.
- **Python packages:** `pip3 install <package>` — no `--break-system-packages` flag needed.
